\begin_layout Title
Chapter 3: Consumer Theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Need to complete: Proposition 46, Example 1, Problem 5
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
3.8
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Aggregate demand
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For most questions in economics, the aggregate behavior of consumers is
 more important the the behavior of any single consumer.
 In this chapter, we investigate the extent to which the theory presented
 in the previous sections can be applied to 
\emph on
aggregate demand
\emph default
, a suitably defined sum of demands arising from all the economy's consumers.
 There are, in fact, a number of different properties of individual demand
 that we might hope would also hild in the aggregate.
 Which ones we are interested in at any given moment depend on the particular
 application at hand.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this section, we ask three quesitons about aggregate demand:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Individual demand can be expressed as a function of prices and the individual's
 wealth level.
 
\emph on
When can aggregate demand be expressed as a function of prices and aggregate
 wealth?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Individual demand derived from rational preferences necessarily satisfies
 the weak axiom of revealed preference.
 
\emph on
When does aggregate demand satisfy the weak axiom?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Individual demand has welfare significance; from it, we can derive measures
 of welfare change for the consumer.
 
\emph on
When does aggregate demand have welfare significance?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Although the conditions we identify as important for each of these aggregation
 questions are closely related, the questions being asked in the three cases
 are conceptually quite distinct.
 Overall, we shall see that, in all three cases, very strong restrictions
 will need to hold for the desired aggregation properties to obtain.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now consider a multi-agent model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
There are 
\begin_inset Formula $L\in N$
\end_inset

 commodities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
There is a set of agents 
\begin_inset Formula $N\equiv\{1,\ldots,n\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Agents consumer bundles of commodities in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's preferences are denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We assume that the demand correspondence induced by each 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{i}$
\end_inset

 is single-valued, with demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.8.1 Aggregate demand function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(90).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 be a profile of demand functions.
 The 
\series bold
summation of demands
\series default
 associated with this profile is the function:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
SD: & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}^{N} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\\
 & (p,w^{1},\ldots,w^{n}) & \mapsto & SD(p,w^{1},\ldots,w^{n})\equiv\sum_{i\in N}x^{i}(p,w^{i})
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus, the summation of demands depends not only on prices but also on the
 specific wealth levels of the various consumers.
 There are two conditions under which we can talk about an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

aggregate demand function
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 instead of the summation of demands.
 Our first condition requires the summation of demand depend only on prices
 and aggregate wealth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(91).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 be a profile of demand functions and 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 its associated summation of demands.
 We say that 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 is an 
\series bold
aggregate demand function
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L},\ \forall\ \left\{ w\equiv(w^{i})_{i\in N},\bar{w}\equiv(\bar{w}^{i})_{i\in N}\right\} \subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{N}:\sum_{i\in N}w^{i}=\sum_{i\in N}\bar{w}^{i}\implies SD(p,w)=SD(p,\bar{w})$
\end_inset

.
 For each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and each aggregate wealth level 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 we conveniently denote
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
AD: & \mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\\
 & (p,\mu) & \mapsto & AD(p,\mu)\equiv SD(p,w^{1},\ldots,w^{n})
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $(w^{i})_{i\in N}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{N}$
\end_inset

 is a profile of wealth such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}w^{i}=\mu$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

One special case in which this property holds arises when all consumers
 have identical preferences that are homothetic.
 Another is when all consumers have preferences that are quasilinear with
 respect to the same good.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\emph on
(37).
 
\series default
\emph default
The aggregate demand function is well defined whenever:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Preferences are quasi-linear all with respect to the same commodity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For each 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{i}$
\end_inset

 is homothetic, and for each pair 
\begin_inset Formula $\{i,j\}\subseteq N$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{i}=\succsim^{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{i}$
\end_inset

 is rational, locally non-satiated, and continuous.
 Take two profiles of wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $w\equiv\left(w^{i}\right)_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{w}\equiv\left(\bar{w}^{i}\right)_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ w^{i},\bar{w}^{i}\right\} \subseteq\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}w^{i}=\sum_{i\in N}\bar{w}^{i}=\mu$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{i}$
\end_inset

 is locally non-satiated, then the profile of demand functions, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(x^{i}\right)_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

, satisfies Walras' Law.
 For both cases, we want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $SD(p,w)=SD(p,\bar{w})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Case 1: Preferences are quasi-linear all with respect to the same commodity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N,$
\end_inset

 let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{i}$
\end_inset

 be quasi-linear with respect to commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
 Consider commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $l\neq k$
\end_inset

.
 Agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's Marshallian demand for good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 is the function, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{l}^{M^{i}}$
\end_inset

, defined by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
x_{l}^{M^{i}}: & \mathbb{R}_{++}^{L} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}_{+}\\
 & p & \mapsto & x_{l}^{M^{i}}(p)\equiv x_{l}^{i}(p,w^{i})\ \mbox{for some}\ w^{i}\in\mathbb{R}_{++}
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

By Proposition 35, since preferences are continuous and locally non-satiated,
 then agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's Marshallian demand for good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 is well defined, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ w^{i},\bar{w}^{i}\right\} \subseteq\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{l}^{i}\left(p,w^{i}\right)=x_{l}^{i}\left(p,\bar{w}^{i}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 As a result, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{l\ne k}x_{l}^{i}\left(p,w^{i}\right)=\sum_{l\ne k}x_{l}^{i}\left(p,\bar{w}^{i}\right)\iff\sum_{l\ne k}p_{l}x_{l}^{i}\left(p,w^{i}\right)=\sum_{l\ne k}p_{l}x_{l}^{i}\left(p,\bar{w}^{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset

 Since Walras' Law holds, then we can find an equation for the demand of
 commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{k}^{i}(p,w^{i})=\frac{w^{i}}{p_{k}}-\frac{\sum_{l\ne k}p_{l}\cdot x_{l}^{i}\left(p,w^{i}\right)}{p_{k}}
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{k}^{i}\left(p,\bar{w}^{i}\right)=\frac{\bar{w}^{i}}{p_{k}}-\frac{\sum_{l\ne k}p_{l}\cdot x_{l}^{i}\left(p,\bar{w}^{i}\right)}{p_{k}}
\]

\end_inset

Now, since 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{l\ne k}p_{l}\cdot x_{l}^{i}\left(p,w^{i}\right)=\sum_{l\ne k}p_{l}\cdot x_{l}^{i}\left(p,\bar{w}^{i}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}w^{i}=\sum_{i\in N}\bar{w}^{i}=\mu$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\sum_{i\in N}x_{k}^{i}(p,w^{i})-\sum_{i\in N}x_{k}^{i}\left(p,\bar{w}^{i}\right) & = & \sum_{i\in N}\frac{w^{i}}{p_{k}}-\sum_{i\in N}\frac{\bar{w}^{i}}{p_{k}}\\
 & = & \frac{\mu}{p_{k}}-\frac{\mu}{p_{k}}=0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}x_{k}^{i}(p,w^{i})=\sum_{i\in N}x_{k}^{i}(p,\bar{w}^{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $SD(p,w)=SD(p,\bar{w})$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $AD(p,\mu)$
\end_inset

 is well defined.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Case 2: For each 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{i}$
\end_inset

 is homothetic, and for each pair 
\begin_inset Formula $\{i,j\}\subseteq N$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{i}=\succsim^{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

By Theorem 10, a continuous preference 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{i}$
\end_inset

 is homothetic if and only if it admits a continuous representation 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{i}:X\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 that is homogeneous of degree one, i.e., such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+}:u^{i}(\alpha x)=\alpha u^{i}(x)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{i,j\}\subseteq N:\ \succsim^{i}=\succsim^{j}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N:u^{i}(x)=u(x)$
\end_inset

.
 This implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N:x^{i}(p,w^{i})=x(p,w^{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

, and take 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in B(p,w^{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w^{i})\in C^{\succsim}(p,w^{i})$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w^{i})\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
 Now, consider 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\frac{1}{w^{i}}\right)y\in B(p,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is a preference maximizing demand function, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,1)\succsim\left(\frac{1}{w^{i}}\right)y$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{i}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,1)\succsim\left(\frac{1}{w^{i}}\right)y\iff u\left(x(p,1)\right)\geq u\left(\left(\frac{1}{w^{i}}\right)y\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Multiplying both sides by 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 yields 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{i}u\left(x(p,1)\right)\geq w^{i}u\left(\left(\frac{1}{w^{i}}\right)y\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is homogenous of degree one, then 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{i}u\left(x(p,1)\right)\geq w^{i}u\left(\left(\frac{1}{w^{i}}\right)y\right)\iff u\left(w^{i}x^{i}(p,1)\right)\geq u\left(y\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{i}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $u\left(w^{i}x^{i}(p,1)\right)\geq u\left(y\right)\iff w^{i}\cdot x(p,1)\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ y\in B(p,w^{i}):w^{i}\cdot x(p,1)\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
 Since the demand correspondence induced by each 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{i}$
\end_inset

 is single-valued, then we must have 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w^{i})=w^{i}\cdot x(p,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 was an arbitrary agent from the set 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

, then this result holds 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N$
\end_inset

.
 Hence,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
SD(p,w)\equiv\sum_{i\in N}x^{i}(p,w^{i})=\sum_{i\in N}x(p,w^{i})=\sum_{i\in N}w^{i}\cdot x(p,1)=\mu\cdot x(p,1)
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
SD(p,\bar{w})\equiv\sum_{i\in N}x^{i}(p,\bar{w}^{i})=\sum_{i\in N}x(p,\bar{w}^{i})=\sum_{i\in N}\bar{w}^{i}\cdot x(p,1)=\mu\cdot x(p,1)
\]

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $SD(p,w)=SD(p,\bar{w})$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $AD(p,\mu)$
\end_inset

 is well defined.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark*
The conditions in Proposition 37 are sufficient for the aggregate demand
 function to be well-defined, but they are not necessary.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(38).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 be a profile of differentiable demand functions and 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 its associated summation of demands.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 is an aggregate demand function if and only if for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

, each wealth vector 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{n}$
\end_inset

, and each 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}v_{i}=0$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{w}SD(p,w)v=0
\]

\end_inset

which is the direction derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $SD(p,w)$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 in the direction of 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 is just a redistribution of wealth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 be a profile of differentiable demand functions and 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 its associated summation of demands.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Rightarrow)$
\end_inset

 Suppose that for each pair of vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{w}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}w^{i}=\sum_{i\in N}\bar{w}^{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $SD(p,w)=SD(p,\bar{w})$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 is an aggregate demand function.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 be such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}v_{i}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $w\gg0$
\end_inset

 be such that 
\begin_inset Formula $w+v\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 Define the following function:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
\Phi: & [0,1] & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\\
 & \alpha & \mapsto & \Phi(\alpha)\equiv SD(p,w+\alpha v)
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

We claim that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \alpha\in[0,1]:\Phi(\alpha)=\Phi(0)$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{w}=w+\alpha v$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}v^{i}=0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}\bar{w}^{i}=\sum_{i\in N}w^{i}+\alpha\sum_{i\in}v^{i}=\sum_{i\in}w^{i}$
\end_inset

.
 By our supposition, 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 is an aggregate demand function, so 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)=S(p,\bar{w})$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi(\alpha)=\Phi(0)$
\end_inset

, which implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is a constant function.
 Thus, taking the derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi(\alpha)$
\end_inset

 and evaluating it 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=0$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\Phi(\alpha)=x^{1}(p,w^{1}+\alpha v^{1})+\cdots+x^{n}(p,w^{n}+\alpha v^{n})=SD(p,w+\alpha v)\\
\Phi^{\prime}(\alpha)=\frac{\partial x^{1}(p,w^{1}+\alpha v^{1})}{\partial w^{1}}v^{1}+\cdots+\frac{\partial x^{n}(p,w^{n}+\alpha v^{n})}{\partial w^{n}}v^{n}=0
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

or in matrix notation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{w}SD(p,w)v=0
\]

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Leftarrow)$
\end_inset

 Suppose that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}v^{i}=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{w}SD(p,w)v=0$
\end_inset

.
 Define the following function:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
\varphi: & [0,1] & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\\
 & \alpha & \mapsto & \varphi(\alpha)\equiv SD(p,\alpha w+(1-\alpha)\bar{w})
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

Application of the chain rule yields the following result: 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \alpha\in[0,1]:\varphi^{\prime}(\alpha)=D_{w}SD(p,\alpha w+(1-\alpha)\bar{w})(w-\bar{w})$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $v\equiv w-\bar{w}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}v^{i}=0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi^{\prime}(\alpha)=0$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi$
\end_inset

 is a constaint function.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi(1)=\varphi(0)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $SD(p,w)=SD(p,\bar{w})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark*
Important to note, the 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 in Proposition 38 is just a redistribution of wealth, while 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{w}SD(p,w)v$
\end_inset

 is a directional derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $SD(p,w)$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 in the direction of 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

.
 In the first part of the proof above, a directional derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 in the direction of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is defined as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\cdot u=Df(x)[u]=\left[\frac{d}{da}f(v+\alpha u)\right]_{\alpha=0}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Corollary*

\series bold
\shape up
(9).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 be a profile of differentiable demand functions and 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 its associated summation of demands.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 is an aggregate demand function if and only if for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

, each pair of agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\{i,j\}\subseteq N$
\end_inset

, and each pair of individual wealth levels 
\begin_inset Formula $\{w^{i},w^{j}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial x^{i}(p,w)}{\partial w^{i}}=\frac{\partial x^{i}(p,w^{j})}{\partial w^{j}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 be a profile of differentiable demand functions and 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 its associated summation of demands.
 By Proposition 38, 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 is an aggregate demand function if and only if for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

, each wealth vector 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{n}$
\end_inset

, and each 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}v_{i}=0$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{w}SD(p,w)v=0
\]

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $v\equiv(0,\ldots,0,1,0,\ldots,0,-1,0,\ldots,0)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 is in the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

-th position and 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 is in the 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

-th position.
 Then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
D_{w}SD(p,w)v & = & 0\\
\frac{\partial x^{i}(p,w^{i})}{\partial w^{i}}-\frac{\partial x^{j}(p,w^{j})}{\partial w^{j}} & = & 0\\
\frac{\partial x^{i}(p,w^{i})}{\partial w} & = & \frac{\partial x^{j}(p,w^{j})}{\partial w}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 is an aggregate demand function if and only if for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

, each pair of agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\{i,j\}\subseteq N$
\end_inset

, and each pair of individual wealth levels 
\begin_inset Formula $\{w^{i},w^{j}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial x^{i}(p,w)}{\partial w^{i}}=\frac{\partial x^{i}(p,w^{j})}{\partial w^{j}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In short, for any fixed price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

, and any commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

, the wealth effect at 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 must be the same whatever consumer we look at and whatever his wealth.
 It is indeed farily intuitive that in this case, the individual demand
 changes arising from any wealth redistribution across consumers will cancel
 out.
 Geometrically, the conditions is equivalent to the statement that all consumers
' wealth expansion paths are parallel, straight lines.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma*

\shape up
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 be a profile of differentiable demand functions and 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 its associated summation of demands.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 is an aggregate demand function if and only if for each agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

, each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

, and each wealth vector 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{n}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x^{i}(p,w^{i})=f^{i}(p)+g(p)w^{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $f^{i}(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $g(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 are vector-valued functions of the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 be a profile of differentiable demand functions and 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 its associated summation of demands.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Rightarrow)$
\end_inset

 Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 is an aggregate demand function.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{w,\bar{w}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{N}$
\end_inset

 such that such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}w^{i}=\sum_{i\in N}\bar{w}^{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 is an aggregate demand function, then 
\begin_inset Formula $SD(p,w)=SD(p,\bar{w})$
\end_inset

, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{i\in N}x^{i}(p,w^{i})=\sum_{i\in N}x^{i}(p,\bar{w}^{i})
\]

\end_inset

So, for any 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x^{k}(p,w^{k})+\sum_{i\neq k}x^{i}(p,w^{i})={\displaystyle x}^{j}(p,w^{j})+\sum_{i\neq j}x^{i}(p,\bar{w}^{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

By keeping the total wealth equal on both sides, we can obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x^{k}(p,w^{k}+\epsilon)+\sum_{i\neq k}x^{i}(p,w^{i})={\displaystyle x}^{j}(p,w^{j}+\epsilon)+\sum_{i\neq j}x^{i}(p,\bar{w}^{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Now, subtracting equation (1) from equation (2) and dividing by 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

 yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{x^{k}(p,w^{k}+\epsilon)-x^{k}(p,w^{k})}{\epsilon}=\frac{x^{j}(p,w^{j}+\epsilon)-x^{j}(p,w^{j})}{\epsilon}
\]

\end_inset

which is the definition of the partial derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{i}$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{i}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial x^{k}(p,w^{k})}{\partial w^{k}}=\frac{\partial x^{j}(p,w^{j})}{\partial w^{j}}\equiv g(p)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\cdot):\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, by the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, we must have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x^{i}(p,w^{i})=f^{i}(p)+g(p)w^{i}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Leftarrow)$
\end_inset


\emph on
 
\emph default
Suppose for each agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

, each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

, and each wealth vector 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{n}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{i}(p,w^{i})=f^{i}(p)+g(p)w^{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{w,\bar{w}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{N}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}w^{i}=\sum_{i\in N}\bar{w}^{i}$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{i\in N}x^{i}(p,w^{i})=\sum_{i\in N}x^{i}(p,\bar{w}^{i})
\]

\end_inset

 By our supposition, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\sum_{i\in N}x^{i}(p,w^{i}) & = & \sum_{i\in N}f^{i}(p)+g(p)w^{i}\\
 & = & \sum_{i\in N}f^{i}(p)+g(p)\sum_{i\in}w^{i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}w^{i}=\sum_{i\in N}\bar{w}^{i}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\sum_{i\in N}x^{i}(p,\bar{w}^{i}) & = & \sum_{i\in N}f^{i}(p)+g(p)\sum_{i\in N}\bar{w}^{i}\\
 & = & \sum_{i\in N}f^{i}(p)+g(p)\sum_{i\in N}w^{i}\\
 & = & \sum_{i\in N}x^{i}(p,\bar{w}^{i})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $SD(p,w)=SD(p,\bar{w})$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 is an aggregate demand function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(39).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 be a profile of differentiable demand functions and 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 its associated summation of demands.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 is an aggregate demand function if and only if there is a profile of utility
 representations 
\begin_inset Formula $(u^{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 such that the associated indirect utility functions have the Gorman form
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(p,w^{i})\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}\mapsto V^{u^{i}}(p,w^{i})=a^{i}(p)+b(p)w^{i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 be a profile of differentiable demand functions and 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 its associated summation of demands.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Rightarrow)$
\end_inset

 Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 is an aggregate demand function.
 By Proposition 30, the money metric representation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.
 So, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u^{i}(x^{i}(p,w^{i}))=p\cdot h^{i}(p,x^{i}(p,w^{i}))
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{i}(p,w^{i})=h^{i}(p,x^{i}(p,w^{i}))$
\end_inset

 by Corollary 5 (i.e., cost minimization duality), then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u^{i}(x^{i}(p,w^{i}))=p\cdot x^{i}(p,w^{i})
\]

\end_inset

By the Lemma above, 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 is a an aggregate demand function if and only if for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

, and for each 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{N}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x^{i}(p,w^{i})=f^{i}(p)+g(p)w^{i}
\]

\end_inset

Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u^{i}(x^{i}(p,w^{i})) & = & p\cdot\left[f^{i}(p)+g(p)w^{i}\right]\\
 & = & p\cdot f^{i}(p)+p\cdot g(p)w^{i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Define 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{i}(p)\equiv p\cdot f^{i}(p)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b(p)\equiv p\cdot g(p)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u^{i}(x^{i}(p,w^{i}))=a^{i}(p)+b(p)w^{i}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Leftarrow)$
\end_inset

 Suppose there is profile of utility representations 
\begin_inset Formula $(u^{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 such that the associated indirect utility functions have the form
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(p,w^{i})\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}\mapsto V^{u^{i}}(p,w^{i})=a^{i}(p)+b(p)w^{i}
\]

\end_inset

Take 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{w,\bar{w}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{N}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}w^{i}=\sum_{i\in N}\bar{w}^{i}$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{i\in N}x^{i}(p,w^{i})=\sum_{i\in N}x^{i}(p,\bar{w}^{i})
\]

\end_inset

By Proposition 29 (Roy's Identity), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x^{i}(p,w^{i}) & = & -\frac{1}{\nabla_{w^{i}}V^{u^{i}}(p,w^{i})}\nabla_{p}V^{u^{i}}(p,w^{i})\\
 & = & -\frac{1}{b(p)}\nabla_{p}a^{i}(p)-\frac{w^{i}}{b(p)}\nabla_{p}b(p)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Equivalently,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x^{i}(p,\bar{w}^{i})=-\frac{1}{b(p)}\nabla_{p}a^{i}(p)-\frac{\bar{w}^{i}}{b(p)}\nabla_{p}b(p)
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}w^{i}=\sum_{i\in N}\bar{w}^{i}$
\end_inset

, then we must have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{i\in N}x^{i}(p,w^{i})=\sum_{i\in N}x^{i}(p,\bar{w}^{i})
\]

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $SD(p,w)=SD(p,\bar{w})$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $SD$
\end_inset

 is an aggregate demand function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus, aggregate demand can be written as a function of aggregate wealth
 if and only if all consumers have preferences that admit indirect utility
 functions of the Gorman form with equal wealth coefficients 
\begin_inset Formula $b(p)$
\end_inset

.
 Needless to say, this is a very restrictive condition on preferences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Given this conclusion, we might ask whether less restrictive conditions
 can be obtained if we consider aggregate demand functions that depend on
 a wider set of aggregate variables than just the total (or, equivalently,
 the mean) wealth level.
 These more general forms of dependence on the distribution of wealth are
 indeed valid under weaker conditions than those required for aggregate
 demand to depend only on aggregate wealth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Besides the conditions above, we can talk about aggregate demand when we
 assume that society distributes wealth in a way that is independent of
 the agents preferences, that is, depends only on prices and aggregate wealth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(92).

\series default
 
\emph on
A 
\series bold
wealth distribution rule
\series default
 is a function that associates a distribution of wealth with each pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,\mu)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 The generic wealth distribution rule is denoted:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
\omega: & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{N}\\
 & (p,\mu) & \mapsto & \omega(p,\mu)\equiv(\omega^{1}(p,\mu),\ldots,\omega^{n}(p,\mu))
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}\omega^{i}(p,\mu)=\mu$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When individual wealth levels are generated by a wealth distribution rule,
 we can indeed 
\emph on
always
\emph default
 write aggregate demand as a function that depends only on prices and aggregate
 wealth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(93).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 be a profile of demand functions and 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 a wealth distribution rule.
 The 
\series bold
aggregate demand associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 
\series default
is a function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
AD^{\omega}: & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\\
 & (p,\mu) & \mapsto & AD^{\omega}(p,\mu)\equiv\sum_{i\in}x^{i}(p,\omega^{i}(p,\mu))
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark*
While 
\begin_inset Formula $AD$
\end_inset

 may not be well-defined, 
\begin_inset Formula $AD^{\omega}$
\end_inset

 is always well-defined no matter what the profile of preferences 
\begin_inset Formula $(\succsim^{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 looks like.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(94).

\series default
 
\emph on
A wealth distribution rule is 
\series bold
price-independent 
\series default
iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{p,p^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L},\ \forall\ \mu\in\mathbb{R}_{+}:\omega(p,\mu)=\omega(p^{\prime},\mu)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
(A price-independent wealth distribution rule).
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(\alpha_{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 be such that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\equiv\alpha_{i}\geq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}\alpha_{i}=1$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
proportional wealth distribution rule with weights 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset


\series default
 is the function:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(p,\mu)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}\mapsto(\alpha_{1}\mu,\ldots,\alpha_{n}\mu)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.8.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Aggregate demand and WARP
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To what extent do the positive properties of individual demand carry over
 to the aggregate demand function? We can note immediately three properties
 that do: continuity, homogeneity of degree zero, and Walras' law.
 In this subsection, we focus on the conditions under which aggregate demand
 also satisfies the weak axiom, arguably the most central positive property
 of the individual Walrasian demand function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To study this quesiton, we would like to operate on an aggregate demand
 function that depends only on prices and aggregate wealth, so it is, in
 a sense, a market demand function.
 We accomplish this by supposing that there is a wealth distribution rule
 determining individual wealths from the price vector and total wealth.
 In point of fact, and merely for the sake of concreteness, we shall be
 even more specific and focus on cases in which relative wealths are independent
 of prices.
 We now investigate the fulfullment of the weak axiom by the aggregate demand
 function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the rest of this section, we only focus on 
\begin_inset Formula $AD^{\omega}$
\end_inset

, which is the aggregate demand associated with a profile of demands 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 and wealth distribution rule 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

.
 The following example shows that properties of individual demand functions
 are not necessarily transferred into aggregate demand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\series bold
(12).
 
\series default
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $N\equiv\{1,2\}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 the proportional wealth distribution rule with weights 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}=\alpha_{2}=\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $AD^{\omega}$
\end_inset

 may not satisfy WARP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark*
The individual demand functions satisfy WARP, but the aggregate demand functions
 
\begin_inset Formula $AD$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $AD^{\omega}$
\end_inset

 may not.
 Also, 
\begin_inset Formula $AD$
\end_inset

 may not satisfy WARP when preferences are quasi-linear, but 
\begin_inset Formula $AD$
\end_inset

 will satisfy WARP if preferences are homothetic and equal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The example above shows that this inequality may be violated even for proportion
al wealth distribution rules.
 The reason for the failure can be traced to wealth effects.
 Recall that 
\begin_inset Formula $AD^{\omega}$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP if and only if it satisfies the compensated law of demand,
 i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{p,p^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L},\ \forall\ \mu\in\mathbb{R}_{+}:$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left(p^{\prime}-p\right)\cdot\left[AD^{\omega}\left(p^{\prime},p^{\prime}\cdot AD^{\omega}(p,\mu)\right)-AD^{\omega}(p,\mu)\right]\leq0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

with strict inequality when 
\begin_inset Formula $AD^{\omega}\left(p^{\prime},p^{\prime}\cdot AD^{\omega}(p,\mu)\right)\neq AD^{\omega}(p,\mu)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If the price-wealth change under consideration, say from 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

, happened to be a compensated price change for 
\emph on
every
\emph default
 consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, then because individual demand satisfies the weak axiom, adding over 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 gives precisely (3).
 Thus, we conclude that aggregate demand must satisfy the weak axiom for
 any price-wealth change that is compensated for every consumer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The difficulty arises because a price-wealth change that is compensated
 in the aggregate, need not be compensated for each individual.
 If so, the individual wealth effects [which, except for certain conditions,
 are essentially unrestricted] can play havoc with the well-behaved but
 possibly small individual substitution effects.
 The result may be that the compensated law of demand fails to hold for
 some 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, thus making possible the failure in the aggregate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Given that a property of individual demand as basic as the weak axiom cannot
 be expected to hold generally for aggregate demand, we mught wish to know
 whether there are any restrictions on individual preferences under which
 it must be satisfied.
 The preceding discussion suggests that it may be worth exploring the implicaito
ns of assuming that the law of demand holds at the individual level for
 price changes that are left uncompensated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(95).

\series default
 
\emph on
A demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies the 
\series bold
uncompensated law of demand 
\series default
iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{p,p^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L},\ \forall\ \mu\in\mathbb{R}_{+}:$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(p^{\prime}-p\right)\cdot\left[x(p^{\prime},\mu)-x(p,\mu)\right]\leq0
\]

\end_inset

with strict inequality when 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},\mu)\neq x(p,\mu)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In view of our discussion of the weak axiom in previous sections, the following
 differential version of the ULD property should come as no surprise:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{i}(p,w^{i})$
\end_inset

 satisfies the ULD property, then 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}x^{i}(p,w^{i})$
\end_inset

 is negative semidefinite; that is, 
\begin_inset Formula $dp\cdot D_{p}x^{i}(p,w^{i})dp\leq0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $dp$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As with the weak axiom, there is a converse to this:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}x^{i}(p,w^{i})$
\end_inset

 is negative definite for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{i}(p,w^{i})$
\end_inset

 satisfies the ULD property.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The analogous differential version holds for the aggregate demand function.
 The great virtue of the ULD property is that, in contrast with the weak
 axiom, it does, in fact, aggregate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(40).

\series default
 
\emph on
Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 is price-independent.
 If for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{i}$
\end_inset

 satisfies the uncompensated law of demand, then 
\begin_inset Formula $AD^{\omega}$
\end_inset

 satisfies the uncompensated law of demand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{p,p^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $w\equiv\omega(p,\mu)=\omega(p^{\prime},\mu)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 is price-independent, then 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 is well-defined.
 We prove that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(p^{\prime}-p)\cdot\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i\in N}}x^{i}(p^{\prime},w^{i})-{\displaystyle \sum_{i\in N}}x^{i}(p,w^{i})\right)\leq0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

with strict inequality when 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}x^{i}(p^{\prime},w^{i})\neq\sum_{i\in}x^{i}(p,w^{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Clearly, we can write the inequality above as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{i\in N}}(p^{\prime}-p)\cdot\left(x^{i}(p^{\prime},w^{i})-x^{i}(p,w^{i})\right)\leq0
\]

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{i}$
\end_inset

 satisfies ULD, then 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{\prime}-p)\cdot\left(x^{i}(p^{\prime},w^{i})-x^{i}(p,w^{i})\right)\leq0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the weak inequality (3) holds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now if 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}x^{i}(p^{\prime},w^{i})\neq\sum_{i\in}x^{i}(p,w^{i})$
\end_inset

, then it must be the case that for some 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{i}(p^{\prime},w^{i})\neq x^{i}(p,w^{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(p^{\prime}-p)\cdot\left(x^{i}(p^{\prime},w^{i})-x^{i}(p,w^{i})\right)<0$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, the strict inequality in (3) holds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(41).

\series default
 
\emph on
If a demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law and the uncompensated law of demand, then it satisfies
 WARP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{p,p^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 We prove that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(p^{\prime}-p)\cdot\left(x(p^{\prime},p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w))-x(p,w)\right)<0
\]

\end_inset

when 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w))\neq x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot x(p^{\prime},p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w))=p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 then the inequality above is equivalent to
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-p\cdot\left(x(p^{\prime},p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w))-x(p,w)\right)<0
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{\prime}\equiv p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose without loss of generality that 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{\prime}>0$
\end_inset

, for otherwise the problem is trivial.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=\frac{w}{w^{\prime}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{p}\equiv\alpha p^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is homogenous of degree zero, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w))=x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})=x(p^{\prime},\frac{w}{\alpha})=x(\tilde{p},w)$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $x(\tilde{p},w)\neq x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies the ULD, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(\tilde{p}-p)\cdot\left(x(\tilde{p},w)-x(p,w)\right)<0
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{p}\cdot x(\tilde{p},w)=w$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{p}\cdot x(p,w)=\frac{w}{w^{\prime}}p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)=w$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-p\cdot\left(x(\tilde{p},w)-x(p,w)\right)<0
\]

\end_inset

Equivalently,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-p\cdot\left(x(p^{\prime},p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w))-x(p,w)\right)<0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Corollary*

\series bold
\shape up
(10).

\series default
 
\emph on
Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 is price-independent and for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{i}$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law and the uncompensated law of demand.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $AD^{\omega}$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 is price-independent and for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{i}$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law and the uncompensated law of demand.
 So, by Proposition 40, 
\begin_inset Formula $AD^{\omega}$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law and the uncompensated law of demand.
 Thus, by Proposition 41, 
\begin_inset Formula $AD^{\omega}$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

How restrictive is the ULD property as an axiom of individual behavior?
 It is clearly not implied by preference maximization.
 Propositions 42 and 44 provide sufficient conditions for individual demands
 to satisfy the ULD property.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\emph on
(42).

\series default
 
\emph default
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be a twice differentiable demand function.
 If for each pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,\mu)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}x(p,\mu)$
\end_inset

 is negative definite, then the demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies the uncompensated law of demand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be a twice differentiable demand function.
 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ (p,\mu)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}x(p,\mu)$
\end_inset

 is negative definite.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{p,p^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\neq p$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 We prove that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(p^{\prime}-p\right)\cdot\left[x(p^{\prime},\mu)-x(p,\mu)\right]<0
\]

\end_inset

when 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)\neq x(p^{\prime},w)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \lambda\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

, define
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(\lambda)=\lambda p^{\prime}+(1-\lambda)p
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mu(\lambda)=\left(p^{\prime}-p\right)\cdot\left[x(p(\lambda),\mu)-x(p,\mu)\right]
\]

\end_inset

So, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mu(0)=\left(p^{\prime}-p\right)\cdot\left[x(p,\mu)-x(p,\mu)\right]=0
\]

\end_inset

since 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,\mu)-x(p,\mu)=0$
\end_inset

.
 By the Chain Rule, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \lambda\in[0,1]$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mu^{\prime}(\lambda) & = & \left(p^{\prime}-p\right)^{T}D_{p}x(p(\lambda),\mu))p^{\prime}(\lambda)\\
 & = & \left(p^{\prime}-p\right)^{T}D_{p}x(p(\lambda),\mu))(p^{\prime}-p)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}x(p,\mu)$
\end_inset

 is negative definite and 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\neq p$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \lambda\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu^{\prime}(\lambda)<0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mu(1)=\left(p^{\prime}-p\right)\cdot\left[x(p^{\prime},w)-x(p,\mu)\right]<0
\]

\end_inset

Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 satisfies the uncompensated law of demand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(43).

\series default
 
\emph on
If a twice differentiable demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies the uncompensated law of demand, then for each 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,\mu)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}x(p,\mu)$
\end_inset

 is negative semi-definite.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is a twice differentiable demand function that satisfies the uncompensated
 law of demand.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,\mu)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 We will prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}x(p,\mu)$
\end_inset

 is negative semi-definite.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

By the definition of a directional partial derivative,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p}x(p,w)dp={\displaystyle \lim_{\epsilon\rightarrow0}}\frac{x(p+\epsilon dp,w)-x(p,w)}{\epsilon}
\]

\end_inset

Hence
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
dp\cdot D_{p}x(p,w)dp & = & dp\cdot\left[{\displaystyle \lim_{\epsilon\rightarrow0}}\frac{x(p+\epsilon dp,w)-x(p,w)}{\epsilon}\right]\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \lim_{\epsilon\rightarrow0}}\frac{dp\cdot\left[x(p+\epsilon dp,w)-x(p,w)\right]}{\epsilon}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Note that the ULD property implies that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
dp\cdot\left[x(p+\epsilon dp,w)-x(p,w)\right]\leq0
\]

\end_inset

for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, by taking the limit 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

, we obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
dp\cdot D_{p}x(p,w)dp\leq0
\]

\end_inset

Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is negative semidefinite.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(44).
 
\series default
\emph on
Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is homothetic and its associated demand correspondence is single-valued
 with demand 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies the ULD.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
We will consider the differentiable case, so we assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is differentiable and that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is representable by a differentiable utility function.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is homothetic, then from the proof of Proposition 37, 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)=w\cdot x(p,1)$
\end_inset

.
 The matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
D_{p}x(p,w) & = & S(p,w)-D_{w}x(p,w)x(p,w)^{T}\\
 & = & S(p,w)-D_{w}\left[w\cdot x(p,1)\right]x(p,w)^{T}\\
 & = & S(p,w)-x(p,1)x(p,w)^{T}\\
D_{p}x(p,w) & = & S(p,w)-\frac{1}{w}x(p,w)x(p,w)^{T}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is the consumer's Slutsky matrix.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[dp\cdot x(p,w)\right]^{2}>0$
\end_inset

 except when 
\begin_inset Formula $dp\cdot x(p,w)=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $dp\cdot S(p,w)dp<0$
\end_inset

 except when 
\begin_inset Formula $dp$
\end_inset

 is proportional to 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

, we can conclude that 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is negative definite.
 Thus, by Proposition 42, the ULD condition holds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(45).

\series default
 
\emph on
Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is a homothetic preference relation such that for each pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,\mu)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

 and each vector 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

 that is not multiple scalar of 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $v^{T}S(p,\mu)v<0$
\end_inset

.
 Then, for each pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,\mu)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}x(p,\mu)$
\end_inset

 is negative definite and thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies the uncompensated law of demand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
This proposition is a direct consequence of Proposition 44, so its proof
 will not be given.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In Proposition 44, the conclusion is obtained with minimal help from the
 substitution effects.
 Those could all be arbitrarily small.
 The wealth effects by themselves turn out to be sufficiently well behaved.
 Unfortunately, the homothetic case is the only one in which this is so.
 More generally, for the ULD property to hold, the substitution effects
 (which are always well behaved) must be large enough to overcome possible
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

perversities
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 coming from the wealth effects.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The condition in Proposition 42 is not an extremely stringent one.
 In particular, notice how amply the homothetic case fits into it.
 So, to the quesiton 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

How restrictive is the ULD property as an axiom of individual behavior?
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 perhaps we can answer: 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

restrictive, but not extremely so.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Not to misrepresent the import of this claim, we should emphasize that Propositi
on 40, which asserts that the ULD property is preserved under addition,
 holds for the price-independent distribution rules that we are considering
 in this section.
 When the distribution of real wealth may depend on prices (as it typically
 will in the general equilibrium applications), then aggregate demand may
 violate the weak axiom even if individual demand satisfies the ULD property.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note, in addition, that for the ULD property to hold for aggregate demand,
 it is not necessary that the ULD be satisfied at the individual level.
 It may arise out of aggregation itself.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(46).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 be the proportional wealth distribution rule with weights 
\begin_inset Formula $(\alpha_{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

.
 For each 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

 and each pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,\mu)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, let 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{i}(p,\mu)$
\end_inset

 be the Slutsky matrix associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{i}$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,\mu)$
\end_inset

.
 Then for each 
