\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.8.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Aggregate demand and welfare
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The aggregation question we pose in this subsection is: When can we compute
 meaningful measures of aggregate welfare using the aggregate demand function
 and the welfare measurement techniques for individual consumers? More specifica
lly, when can we treat the aggregate demand function as if it were generated
 by a fictional 
\emph on
representative consumer
\emph default
 whose preferences can be used as a measure of aggregate societal (or 
\emph on
social
\emph default
) welfare?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is useful to begin by distinguishing two senses in which we could say
 that there is a representative consumer.
 The first is a positive, or behavioral, sense.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A positive representative consumer can be thought of as a fictional individual
 whose utility maximization problem when facing society's budget set 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:p\cdot x\leq\mu\}$
\end_inset

 would generate the economy's aggregate demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $AD^{\omega}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For it to be correct to treat aggregate demand as we did individual demand
 functions, there must be a positive representative consumer.
 However, although this is a necessary condition for the property of aggregate
 demand that we seek, it is not sufficient.
 We also need to be able to assign welfare significance to this ficitonal
 individual's demand function.
 This will lead to the definition of a 
\emph on
normative
\emph default
 representative consumer.
 To do so, however, we first have to be more specific about what we mean
 by the term 
\emph on
social welfare
\emph default
.
 We accomplish this by introducing the concept of a 
\emph on
social welfare function
\emph default
, a function that provides a summary (social) utility index for any collection
 of individual utilities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(96).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim\equiv(\succsim_{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 be a profile of preferences.
 A 
\series bold
social welfare pair for 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset


\series default
 is a pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(u,W)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $u=(u_{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 is a profile of representations for 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 is a function from 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{N}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
social weflare order associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $(u,W)$
\end_inset

 
\series default
is a complete and transitive binary relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L\times N}$
\end_inset

 represented by the function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
W: & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L\times N} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}\\
 & (x^{1},\ldots,x^{n}) & \mapsto & W(u_{1}(x^{1}),\ldots,u_{n}(x^{n}))
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark*
We assume in this subsection that our direct utility functions 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 are concave.
 This is a weak hypothesis (once quasiconcavity has been assumed) which
 makes sure that in all the optimization problems to be considered, the
 first-order conditions are sufficient for the determination of global optimal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The idea behind a social welfare function 
\begin_inset Formula $W(u_{1},\ldots,u_{n})$
\end_inset

 is that it accurately expresses society's judgments on how individual utilities
 have to be compared to produce an ordering of possible social outcomes.
 We also assume that social welfare functions are increasing, concave, and
 whenever convenient, differentiable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let us now hypothesize that there is a process, a benevolent central authority
 perhaps, that, for any given prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and aggregate wealth level 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

, redistributes wealth in order to maximize social welfare.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(47).

\series default
 
\emph on
Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 are the result of the maximization of preferences 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim\equiv(\succsim_{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(u,W)$
\end_inset

 be a social welfare pair for 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega(p,w)$
\end_inset

 solves the problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\max & W(u_{1}(x^{1}(p,w^{1})),\ldots,u_{n}(x^{n}(p,w^{n}))\\
\mbox{s.t.} & (w^{i})_{i\in N}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{N},\ \sum_{i\in N}w^{i}=w\nonumber 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Then, the aggregate demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $AD^{\omega}$
\end_inset

 is the result of the maximization of the preference represented by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
U: & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}\\
 & x & \mapsto & U(x)\equiv\begin{array}{cc}
\max & W(u_{1}(y^{1}),\ldots,u_{n}(y^{n}))\\
\mbox{s.t.} & \{y^{1},\ldots,y^{n}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L},\ \sum_{i\in N}y^{i}\leq x
\end{array}
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

Moreover, the indirect utility function of (5) is the indirect utility function
 associated with this representation of preferences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
In Excerise 4.D.2 of Mas-Colell, you are asked to establish that 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 does indeed have the properties of an indirect utility function.
 The argument for the proof then consists of using Roy's identity to derive
 a Walrasian demand function from 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(p,w)$
\end_inset

, which we denote by 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{R}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and then establishing that it actually equals 
\begin_inset Formula $AD^{\omega}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We begin by recording the first-order conditions of problem (5) for a given
 value of 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Neglecting boundary solutions, these require that for some 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\geq0$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lambda=\frac{\partial W}{\partial V_{1}^{u}}\frac{\partial V_{1}^{u}}{\partial w^{1}}=\cdots=\frac{\partial W}{\partial V_{n}^{u}}\frac{\partial V_{n}^{u}}{\partial w^{n}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

(For notational convenience, we have omitted the points at which the derivatives
 are evaluated.) Condition (6) simply says that at a socially optimal wealth
 distribution, the social utility of an extra unite of wealth is the same
 irrespective of who gets it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

By Roy's identity, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{R}(p,w)=-\frac{1}{\frac{\partial V^{u}(p,w)}{\partial w}}\nabla_{p}V^{u}(p,w)
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is the value function of problem (5), we know that 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\partial V^{u}}{\partial w}=\lambda$
\end_inset

.
 In addition, for any commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

, the chain rule and (6) - or, equivalently, the evelope theorem - give
 us
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial V^{u}}{p_{l}}={\displaystyle \sum_{i\in N}}\frac{\partial W}{\partial V_{i}^{u}}\frac{\partial V_{i}^{u}}{\partial p_{l}}+\lambda{\displaystyle \sum_{i\in N}}\frac{\partial w^{i}}{\partial p_{l}}={\displaystyle \sum_{i\in N}}\frac{\partial W}{\partial V_{i}^{u}}\frac{\partial V_{i}^{u}}{\partial p_{l}}
\]

\end_inset

where the second equality follows because 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}w^{i}(p,w)=w$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}(\nicefrac{\partial w^{i}}{\partial p_{l}})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, in matrix notation, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\nabla_{p}V^{u}(p,w)={\displaystyle \sum_{i\in N}}\left(\nicefrac{\partial W}{\partial V_{i}^{u}}\right)\nabla_{p}V_{i}^{u}(p,w^{i}(p,w))
\]

\end_inset

Finally, using Roy's identity and the first-order condition (6), we get
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{R}(p,w) & = & -\frac{1}{\lambda}{\displaystyle \sum_{i\in N}}\left[\frac{\lambda}{\nicefrac{\partial V_{i}^{u}}{\partial w^{i}}}\right]\nabla_{p}V_{i}^{u}(p,w^{i}(p,w))\\
 & = & -{\displaystyle \sum_{i\in N}}\left[\frac{1}{\nicefrac{\partial V_{i}^{u}}{\partial w^{i}}}\right]\nabla_{p}V_{i}^{u}(p,w^{i}(p,w))\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{i\in N}}x^{i}(p,w^{i}(p,w))=AD^{\omega}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

as we wanted to show.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(\alpha_{i})_{i\in N}$
\end_inset

 be a a set of weights such that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{i}\geq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i\in N}\alpha_{i}=1$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose that consumers have homothetic preferences and 
\begin_inset Formula $(u,W)$
\end_inset

 is a social welfare pair for these preferences such that the utility functions
 
