\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part G.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To show that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 may not satisfy the Uncompensated Law of Demand, we will construct a quasi-line
ar preference such that the corresponding demand function does not satisfy
 ULD.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)=x_{1}+\varphi(x_{2})$
\end_inset

 where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\varphi(x_{2})=\begin{cases}
1000 & \mbox{if}\ x_{2}=1\\
0 & \mbox{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

For the sake of simplicity, we normalize prices so the price of the first
 commodity is always 1.
 So, let 
\begin_inset Formula $p=(1,1)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}=(1,2)$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $w=2$
\end_inset

.
 Maximizing 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)$
\end_inset

 with respect to the given wealth and each set of prices yields 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,2)=(1,1)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},2)=(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,2)\neq x(p^{\prime},2)$
\end_inset

.
 Now, since 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta p_{1}=0$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
(p^{\prime}-p)\cdot(x(p^{\prime},2)-x(p,2)) & = & \Delta p_{2}\cdot\Delta x_{2}\\
 & = & 1\cdot0\\
 & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 does not satisfy the uncompensated law of demand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Construct a strictly convex quasi-linear preference in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 whose associated demand funciton does not satisfy the ULD.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
This problem is equivalent to Part G of Problem 3.
