\end_inset

 the Uncompensated Law of Demand is.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be the preference on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

 represented by the utility function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(x_{1},x_{2})\equiv\min\{x_{1},x_{2}\}
\]

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\succsim}$
\end_inset

 be the demand function associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
State the Uncompensated Law of Demand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\succsim}$
\end_inset

 satisfies the Uncompensated Law of Demand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Now consider the preference on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{m}$
\end_inset

, represented by the utility function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u^{m}(x_{1},x_{2})\equiv\begin{cases}
\min\{x_{1},x_{2}\} & \mbox{if}\ \min\{x_{1},x_{2}\}\leq1\\
\min\{x_{1},\frac{1}{m}x_{2}+\frac{m-1}{m}\} & \mbox{if}\ \min\{x_{1},x_{2}\}>1
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $m\in(0,+\infty)$
\end_inset

.
 Prove that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $m\neq1$
\end_inset

, the demand function associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{m}$
\end_inset

 violates the Uncompensated Law of Demand.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be the preference on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

 represented by the utility function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(x_{1},x_{2})\equiv\min\{x_{1},x_{2}\}
\]

\end_inset

and let 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\succsim}$
\end_inset

 be the demand function associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\succsim}$
\end_inset

 satisfies the uncompensated law of demand iff for each pair 
\begin_inset Formula $\{p,p^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(p^{\prime}-p)\cdot\left[x^{\succsim}(p^{\prime},\mu)-x^{\succsim}(p,\mu)\right]\leq0
\]

\end_inset

with strict inequality when 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\succsim}(p^{\prime},\mu)\neq x^{\succsim}(p,\mu)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since the preferences are Leontief, then they are also monotone and stricly
 convex, so the demand correspondence is single-valued.
 In addition, the associated demand function satifies Walras' Law.
 The demand function is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x^{\succsim}(p,w)\equiv\left(\frac{w}{p_{1}+p_{2}},\frac{w}{p_{1}+p_{2}}\right)
\]

\end_inset

Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{p,p^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{++}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 Now, consider
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
(p^{\prime}-p)\cdot\left[x^{\succsim}(p^{\prime},w)-x^{\succsim}(p,w)\right] & = & (p_{1}^{\prime}-p_{1})\left[\frac{w}{p_{1}^{\prime}+p_{2}^{\prime}}-\frac{w}{p_{1}+p_{2}}\right]+(p_{2}^{\prime}-p_{2})\left[\frac{w}{p_{1}^{\prime}+p_{2}^{\prime}}-\frac{w}{p_{1}+p_{2}}\right]\\
 & = & \left[\frac{w}{p_{1}^{\prime}+p_{2}^{\prime}}-\frac{w}{p_{1}+p_{2}}\right](p_{1}^{\prime}-p_{1}+p_{2}^{\prime}-p_{2})\\
 & = & w\left[\frac{p_{1}-p_{1}^{\prime}+p_{2}-p_{2}^{\prime}}{(p_{1}^{\prime}+p_{2}^{\prime})(p_{1}+p_{2})}\right](p_{1}^{\prime}-p_{1}+p_{2}^{\prime}-p_{2})\\
 & = & -w\left[\frac{p_{1}+p_{2}-p_{1}^{\prime}-p_{2}^{\prime}}{(p_{1}^{\prime}+p_{2}^{\prime})(p_{1}+p_{2})}\right]^{2}\leq0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

with strict inequality if 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}+p_{2}\neq p_{1}^{\prime}+p_{2}^{\prime}\iff x^{\succsim}(p,w)\neq x^{\succsim}(p^{\prime},w)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the associated demand function of a Leontief preference relation
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

 satisfies the uncompensated law of demand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part C.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The demand function associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{m}$
\end_inset

 is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x(p,w)\equiv\begin{cases}
\left(\frac{w}{p_{1}+p_{2}},\frac{w}{p_{1}+p_{2}}\right) & \mbox{if}\ w\leq p_{1}+p_{2}\\
\left(\frac{w+(m-1)p_{2}}{p_{1}+mp_{2}},\frac{mw-(m-1)p_{1}}{p_{1}+mp_{2}}\right) & \mbox{if}\ w>p_{1}+p_{2}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

To prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 violates the ULD, we will show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta p\cdot\Delta x=0$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x\neq0$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta p=p^{\prime}-p$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x=x(p^{\prime},w)-x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To start, when 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 is large enough, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $w>p_{1}+p_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w>p_{1}^{\prime}+p_{2}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w)$
\end_inset

 should be on the income expansion path which is to the northeast of point
 
\begin_inset Formula $(1,1)$
\end_inset

.
 So, for any two points, 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w)$
\end_inset

, on that expansion path, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\Delta x_{2}}{\Delta x_{1}}=m
\]

\end_inset

Also, from the optimality condition for preference maximization,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\Delta p_{1}}{\Delta p_{2}} & = & -\frac{\Delta x_{2}}{\Delta x_{1}}\\
\frac{\Delta p_{1}}{\Delta p_{2}} & = & -m
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\Delta p_{1}\Delta x_{1}+\Delta p_{2}\Delta x_{2} & = & -m\Delta p_{2}\frac{\Delta x_{2}}{m}+\Delta p_{2}\Delta x_{2}\\
\Delta p_{1}\Delta x_{1}+\Delta p_{2}\Delta x_{2} & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Now, we need to show 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w)\neq x(p,w)$
\end_inset

, which is equivalent to showing that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}(p^{\prime},w)\neq x_{1}(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Note that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{1}(p,w)=\frac{w+(m-1)p_{2}}{p_{1}+mp_{2}}
\]

\end_inset

Suppose for contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}(p,w)=x_{1}(p^{\prime},w)$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{w+(m-1)p_{2}^{\prime}}{p_{1}^{\prime}+mp_{2}^{\prime}}=\frac{w+(m-1)p_{2}}{p_{1}+mp_{2}}\equiv K
\]

\end_inset

Important to note,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{a}{b}=\frac{c}{d}\equiv\frac{a\pm c}{b\pm d}
\]

\end_inset

as long as 
\begin_inset Formula $b\pm d\neq0$
\end_inset

.
 So, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{(m-1)\Delta p_{2}}{\Delta p_{1}+m\Delta p_{2}} & = & K\\
(m-1)\Delta p_{2} & = & Km\Delta p_{2}+K\Delta p_{1}\\
\frac{\Delta p_{1}}{\Delta p_{2}} & = & \frac{(m-1)-Km}{K}\\
\frac{\Delta p_{1}}{\Delta p_{2}} & = & -m+\frac{m-1}{K}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{(m-1)}{K}\neq0$
\end_inset

, then this result contradicts the fact that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\Delta p_{1}}{\Delta p_{2}}=-m
\]

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w)\neq x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 does not satisfy the Uncompensated Law of Demand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark*
If Leontief preferences begin at the origin, then the associated demand
 function satisfies the ULD.
 If Leontief preferences begin at a different location, then the associated
 demand function may not satisfy the ULD.
 Most importantly, the associated demand functions of general Leontief preferenc
es may not satisfy the ULD.
\end_layout

